Option B: administrator account

For admins, OpenSSH ignores the per-user file. The key has to go in a shared file that only Administrators and SYSTEM can access. Run this in an elevated PowerShell:

$f = "$env:ProgramData\ssh\administrators_authorized_keys"
Add-Content -Encoding ascii $f 'ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIE1UwK+SgNfAwcL6DqKF2Nxy+faALrwqAZj6M7SwOcan claude-pdvzip-winvm'
icacls $f /inheritance:r /grant "Administrators:F" /grant "SYSTEM:F"

Then, for either option, start the SSH server and make sure it comes back after reboots (elevated PowerShell):

Start-Service sshd
Set-Service sshd -StartupType Automatic

The OpenSSH installer normally opens port 22 in the firewall. If I can't connect, run New-NetFirewallRule -Name sshd -DisplayName 'OpenSSH Server' -Protocol TCP -LocalPort 22 -Action Allow in the same elevated PowerShell.


